---
// src/components/overlays/MarsOverlay.astro — Labs / Experiments
// 8 sections — layout data-attributes drive CSS positioning.
// Sections match MARS_SCROLL_PATH waypoints in PlanetScrollCameras.ts.

const items = [
  {
    id:       'cognitive-arena',
    category: 'EXPERIMENT',
    title:    'Cognitive Arena',
    meta:     'Fish Pond · Scripted, no AI model',
    body: [
      'Cognitive Arena is a scripted, non-AI simulation inside Fish Pond: a single fish responds to eight different stimuli, some known and some not. Known stimuli trigger fast, reflex-like reactions; unknown objects get a slower, multi-phase appraisal instead.',
      'Short-term memory is visible in real time through a live "Fish Mind" readout — the point of the arena is to make the decision process legible rather than a black box, comparing reflex-like, probabilistic, and deliberative behaviour side by side.',
      'No AI model runs here at all — it is deliberately scripted, which makes it the control case against the Underwater scene\'s actual on-device model.',
    ],
    topics: ['The Setup', 'Known vs. Unknown Stimuli', 'The Control Case'],
  },
  {
    id:       'visual-telemetry-benchmark',
    category: 'EXPERIMENT',
    title:    'Visual Telemetry Benchmark',
    meta:     'Fish Pond · Seeded, 16s cycle',
    body: [
      'Three scripted controllers run side by side on identical inputs, with live telemetry overlaid on each — seeded randomness keeps every run reproducible rather than letting chance mask real differences in behaviour.',
      'Each automated test cycle runs for sixteen seconds before resetting, long enough to see divergence between the controllers without turning the comparison into a long-running observation task.',
      'It exists for the same reason a benchmark exists anywhere else in this body of work: a claim about behaviour is only useful once it is measured against something, not just described.',
    ],
    topics: ['The Setup', 'Why Seeded', 'The 16-Second Cycle'],
  },
  {
    id:       'underwater-laya-ai',
    category: 'EXPERIMENT',
    title:    'Underwater / Laya-AI',
    meta:     'Fish Pond · ONNX Runtime Web',
    body: [
      'The Underwater scene is Fish Pond\'s most ambitious piece: a realistic goldfish environment with caustics, water absorption, and bloom, built on Triforge node-graph materials instead of hand-written GLSL — the same approach used for every planet on this site.',
      'Fish decisions — flee, go for food, where to swim — are made by an on-device model nicknamed Laya-AI, running in the browser via ONNX Runtime Web. The renderer only carries out what the model decides; it has no decision logic of its own.',
      'Laya-AI is opt-in because the download is roughly 524MB and may not run well on phones or low-end laptops. It is a general text classifier repurposed for this task, not a fish-specific model, and System 2 reasoning for the underwater fish is on the roadmap, not yet implemented.',
    ],
    topics: ['The Scene', 'The Model', 'Known Limits'],
  },
  {
    id:       'inkwake-benchmark',
    category: 'BENCHMARK',
    title:    'Inkwake',
    meta:     'Tailwind vs. Bootstrap vs. Strata',
    body: [
      'Inkwake builds the same anime-themed e-commerce landing page three times — once each in Tailwind, Bootstrap 5, and Strata — holding markup intent, copy, and business logic constant so any measured difference traces back to the CSS framework itself.',
      'Each build deploys live and gets Lighthouse-benchmarked under mobile and desktop presets, ten isolated runs per app per mode, reporting medians alongside means to filter out CPU-contention noise from running tests in parallel.',
      'This is the most rigorous experiment in the set precisely because it is not exploratory — it exists to get a real answer to "does my own framework actually cost less," not just to assume it.',
    ],
    topics: ['The Setup', 'The Methodology', 'Why It\'s Different'],
  },
];
